  ],
  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) Sec. 4, new section (a)\n(quote) The administrator of elections shall reject any application for registration that is not accompanied by satisfactory evidence of United States citizenship.\n(section) Sec. 4, new section (a)(1)\n(quote) The number of the applicant’s driver license issued by the department of safety or a driver license, driver certificate, or nonoperating identification license issued by the equivalent governmental agency of another state within the United States if the agency indicates on the applicant’s driver license, driver certificate or nonoperating identification license that the person has provided satisfactory proof of United States citizenship;\n(section) Sec. 4, new section (b)\n(quote) This section shall only apply to new registrations.\n(section) Sec. 3, TCA 2-2-115(b)(2)\n(quote) The printed registration forms shall also include a statement that the applicant shall submit evidence of United States citizenship with the application and that the registrar shall reject the application if no evidence of citizenship is attached.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Sec. 1, TCA 2-2-116(c) oath\n(quote) I, being duly sworn on oath (or affirmation) declare that I am a citizen of the United States, that the above address is my legal residence, and that I plan to remain at such residence for an undetermined period of time.\n(section) Recital\n(quote) WHEREAS, it is the intent of the general assembly that this act not contravene the National Voter Registration Act but rather help further the purposes of that act; now, therefore,\n(other) Sec. 2: 2-2-115(a) 'postal card' changed to 'registration'.\n(other) Sec. 4(a)(2)-(6): other accepted proof: birth certificate photocopy, passport, naturalization documents (a certificate number alone is verified with U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services before the person goes on the rolls), IRCA-established proof, tribal card numbers.\n(other) Sec. 4(c)-(d): proof need not be resubmitted in that county; the administrator notes it in the voter file and may destroy the documents after two years.\n(other) Sec. 5: forms to be used until January 1, 2012; online forms revised as soon as reasonably possible.\n(other) Sec. 6: severability.\n(other) Sec. 7: takes effect on becoming law.\n(other) version: Draft 105763 (filed 2011-02-04): carries slot d (documentary proof of citizenship from every new registrant, rejected otherwise) and slot e (citizen oath, new-registrations-only); slots a-c not stated.\n(other) version: Draft 525152 (dated 2011-12-20): same operative text as 105763; only the header (adds House Bill 324 by Todd) and line layout differ.\n(other) version: The Senate committee amendment and SA0412 (which the official summary suggests turned this into a comparison of the voter database with the department of safety database) are NOT saved; no database-comparison text is available.\n(other) note: The saved Drafts require documents with every NEW registration; the enacted law, per the official summary, instead has the coordinator compare the voter database with the department of safety database (a check against databases, with the proof requirement for a flagged person unknown). The enacted wording is not saved, so slot d quotes are of the FILED bill only and may not describe the law. The House passed the Senate bill in place of its companion (HB 324).",
